<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feurs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Forum Segusiavorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feurs is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> in the Loire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As Forum Segusiavorum it was the chief town of the Gallic Segusiavi under Roman rule, after the foundation of Lugdunum on their land. It gave its name to the medieval county of Forez, which in turn names the plain of the upper Loire around it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/feurs.jpg" alt="Feurs" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feursurbocentro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Feurs was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and its name is a contraction of Forum Segusiavorum, after the forum of the Gallo-Roman town. Ptolemy gives it as the capital of the Gallic Segusiavi, and it appears on the Tabula Peutingeriana. Archaeologists have found a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, sewers, milestones, inscriptions, statuettes and pottery that reflect the importance of the Roman town, which lay near major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a>. The town gave its name to Forez.</p><p>A church dedicated to St Baudile is reported in 960 and a second, dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, in 1001. A castle is reported in 1246. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town had no bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, but a harbour is recorded in 953 and another near Randans in 1060. Feurs, with the County of Forez, was forfeited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon">Constable of Bourbon</a> with his other possessions in 1523 and entered the royal domain in 1531. In 1542 it was attached to the generality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, created by Henry II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>During the French Revolution Feurs was briefly the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> department, from 1793 to 1795. The Revolutionary Court headed by Claude Javogues made many victims there, about 80, almost all executed at the site where Mayor Pierre-Marie Assier erected the Chapel of the Martyrs in 1826. The town lay on one of the first French railways, the Andrézieux to Roanne line opened on 1 August 1832, and workshops building locomotives were set up there. Feurs stands in the Forez plain, between the Monts du Lyonnais to the east and the Forez mountains to the west, and the Lignon du Forez flows into the Loire in the commune. Remains of the Roman forum survive, and a museum of archaeology shows finds from the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feurs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 43 BCE the colony of Lugdunum (Lyon) was founded on their land, and their own chief town became <b>Forum Segusiavorum</b> (Feurs).</blockquote><a href="/state/segusiavi">Segusiavi</a></li></ul></section>
